# Changes the legacy watches make to code Core's own boards share. The
# legacy boards select LEGACY_WATCH; without it every option here keeps
# Core's behaviour.

config LEGACY_WATCH
    bool
    help
      The board is a legacy Pebble watch (Pebble Time, Time Steel, Time
      Round, Pebble 2), outside Core's own range.

menu "Legacy watch changes to shared code"
    depends on LEGACY_WATCH

config LEGACY_BT_PASSKEY_DISPLAY
    bool "LE legacy pairing with a displayed passkey"
    default y
    help
      Answer a passkey display request (LE legacy pairing, as with
      controllers without Secure Connections) with a random passkey for
      the user to type on the phone, and end pairing on actions the watch
      cannot answer. Let a bonded phone pair again while the watch is
      pairable, so keys that no longer match (e.g. after pairing in the
      stock recovery firmware) can be replaced; refuse it otherwise.

config LEGACY_BT_NAME_ON_FIRST_PAIRING
    bool "Keep the phone's name on the first pairing"
    default y
    help
      Store the phone's name read before the bonding existed, and find the
      connection by its resolved identity address when the read completes.

config LEGACY_BT_HOST_RESET_RECOVERY
    bool "Restore the address after a host reset"
    default y
    help
      Set the identity address again when the NimBLE host resets and
      resyncs, and log repeated host resets and advertising attempts
      without an address as a count.

config LEGACY_BT_STOCK_PAIRINGS
    bool "Delete the stock firmware's pairings"
    default y if BT_CONTROLLER_CC2564
    help
      Let the pairings the stock firmware of the CC2564 watches stored, in
      a shorter record layout, be deleted from the settings.

config LEGACY_BT_CONTROLLER_OFF_WHEN_STOPPED
    bool "Hold the Bluetooth controller in shutdown while Bluetooth is off"
    depends on BT_CONTROLLER_CC2564
    default y
    help
      In airplane mode and while the watch lies still (stationary mode) the
      host stops; hold the CC2564 in shutdown then, about 1 uA instead of
      tens in deep sleep, and boot it with its init script (about a second)
      when Bluetooth comes back on.

config LEGACY_ECOMPASS_RTC_CAL
    bool "Keep the compass calibration across restarts"
    depends on SOC_STM32F4 && MAG
    default y
    help
      Keep the compass calibration in the RTC backup registers, as the
      stock firmware did (same registers and layout), so a restart does not
      throw it away: it lasts until the battery runs out or a charger is
      plugged in (which resets it, as before).

config LEGACY_EMOJI_PACK
    bool "Full emoji set from a pack file"
    help
      The board's resource bank holds only a subset of the colour emoji.
      Draw from the /emoji file when it is installed: a resource pack with
      the full emoji fonts that the board's resource map declares as the
      emoji fonts' extensions. The bank's subset serves what the pack
      misses, and everything without the pack.

config LEGACY_FACTORY_RESET_KEEPS_FIRMWARE
    bool "Factory reset wipes the filesystem but keeps the firmware"
    depends on SOC_STM32F4
    help
      Core's factory reset boots the recovery firmware after the wipe, as
      the stock firmware did. On these watches the recovery firmware is
      the stock one and the phone app then installs the stock firmware
      over it, so a factory reset would also undo this firmware. Keep the
      firmware and wipe the filesystem only; a reset during the wipe
      finishes it at the next boot (RTC backup register).

config LEGACY_LANG_PACK_STOCK_LAYOUT
    bool "Read language packs built for the stock firmware"
    default y
    help
      Language packs built for the stock firmware (the community packs the
      phone app offers where Core has none) hold 19 resources, without the
      two Gothic 36 extensions and the LECO fonts Core's layout adds. Read
      their Bitham, Roboto and Droid fonts from where they are, instead of
      two entries off.

config LEGACY_VOICE_ENCODER_IDLE_FREE
    bool "Free the voice encoder between sessions"
    default y
    help
      Give the Speex encoder's kernel heap (over 8 KB) back when a
      dictation or app microphone session ends, instead of keeping it
      from the first session on.

config LEGACY_ASSERT_CALLER_LR
    bool "libc assert() records its caller"
    default y
    help
      Record the caller of a failed libc assert() as the reboot reason's
      address, rather than assert() itself.

endmenu

config BT_LE_IDLE_SLAVE_LATENCY
    int "Slave latency while idle"
    default 30 if LEGACY_WATCH
    default 4
    help
      Connection events the watch may skip at the slow response time
      (and at the middle one, unless BT_LE_MIDDLE_SLAVE_LATENCY says
      otherwise).

config BT_LE_MIDDLE_SLAVE_LATENCY
    int "Slave latency at the middle response time"
    default 2 if LEGACY_WATCH
    default BT_LE_IDLE_SLAVE_LATENCY
    help
      Connection events the watch may skip at the middle response time,
      which music control, file transfers and apps asking for a reduced
      sniff interval request. The stock firmware used 2; at the idle
      latency of the legacy boards (30) each phone message waited up to
      about a second.

config BT_LE_IDLE_INTERVAL_MIN
    int "Minimum connection interval while idle (1.25 ms units)"
    default 24 if LEGACY_WATCH
    default 36

config BT_LE_IDLE_INTERVAL_MAX
    int "Maximum connection interval while idle (1.25 ms units)"
    default 36 if LEGACY_WATCH
    default 48

config LEGACY_HARDLOCKUP_TEST
    bool "Lock up a minute after boot (test of the hard lockup detector)"
    depends on TASK_WDT_HARDLOCKUP && COREDUMP && SOC_STM32F4
    help
      For testing only. A minute after boot, spin on the system work
      queue with the kernel's interrupts locked, which starves the
      watchdog thread, unless an unread core dump is stored: the detector
      writes one, so the watch then boots normally until it is read.

      It does not lock up on the boot after a crash or a hardware
      watchdog reset, whatever the detector did, nor while a firmware
      update runs. Each lockup, and a hardware watchdog reset if the
      detector fails, is a failed boot for the stock bootloader, which
      starts the recovery firmware at the third without 15 minutes of
      running in between: let the watch run 15 minutes after a test.
